PART 3 — EXACTLY TWO ATTACKS, EXACTLY TWELVE TURNS

THIS IS ONE OF THE MOST IMPORTANT STRICT RULES.

Every fighter must have exactly two specific, designated grounded close-range melee attacks supporting Mash Combo.

Call these two attacks A and B.

If the player triggers Mash Combo by repeatedly pressing A, the automatic series is:

Turn	Attack	Effective Base Launch
1	A	0
2	B	0
3	A	0
4	B	0
5	A	0
6	B	0
7	A	0
8	B	0
9	A	0
10	B	0
11	A	0
12	B	Original Base Launch of B

This produces six activations of A and six activations of B.

If the player triggers Mash Combo using B instead, reverse the sequence:

B → A → B → A → B → A → B → A → B → A → B → A.

In that case, the 12th attack uses A’s original Base Launch.

Strict sequence restrictions

* Exactly 12 attack turns.
* Exactly six uses of each designated attack.
* Exactly two distinct moves in the sequence.
* Strict alternation.
* The triggering attack always goes first.
* No third attack can join the series.
* No projectile, summon, technique, Special, or unrelated attack can automatically become part of the sequence.
* The sequence cannot extend beyond turn 12 through continued button presses.
* The sequence must not loop indefinitely.
* Each attack turn must actually execute its assigned attack.
* Every turn must have a valid animation and collision window.

A turn means one complete activation of a designated move, not one animation frame or one individual hit event.

Some existing ALVA attacks contain several authored hits within a single activation. Preserve that distinction.

A 12-turn Mash Combo may therefore produce more than 12 individual hit events when using existing multi-hit attacks.

Never confuse the 12-turn requirement with a requirement to produce exactly 12 damage events.

When the 12th turn finishes, the fighter returns to normal combat.

Do not automatically restart another Mash Combo from button presses that occurred during the active sequence.

