PART 9 — FULL CPU SUPPORT

CPUs MUST UNDERSTAND AND BE ABLE TO PERFORM MASH COMBOS.

This feature is not player-exclusive.

Every CPU-controlled fighter must have access to its designated Mash Combo pair.

The CPU must respect the same eligibility requirements, input detection, attack timing, 12-turn sequence, launch behavior, vulnerability, and interruption rules as a human player.

CPU activation

The CPU must be able to deliberately initiate a Mash Combo by producing five rapid, legitimate presses of the same eligible grounded close-range attack button.

Those presses must pass through the existing CPU input pipeline.

The CPU must not bypass the detector by simply setting the Mash Combo state to active.

Look carefully at Executor.press() and the CPU’s …Pressed edge generation.

The CPU must release the action between presses when needed, exactly as a real input device would.

Its Mash Combo initiation plan must remain active long enough to generate all five presses, even if the initiating normal attack is still playing.

Do not let normal CPU move-readiness logic prevent intentional mash-input attempts merely because the same attack is still active.

Tactical decision-making

Teach the CPU when Mash Combo is strategically appropriate.

The CPU should recognize opportunities such as:

* A nearby grounded opponent.
* An opponent in range of both eligible attacks.
* An opponent who has committed to a punishable move.
* A favorable close-range pressure situation.
* A potential finishing opportunity with the twelfth attack’s launch.

It should also recognize reasons not to mash:

* The opponent is far away.
* The opponent is airborne beyond close-range reach.
* The CPU is dangerously close to an unprotected ledge.
* A nearby hazard makes commitment dangerous.
* The opponent can easily interrupt the series.
* The opponent is consistently defending successfully.
* A more valuable defensive or recovery action is needed.

Difficulty levels

All four difficulty levels must be capable of performing Mash Combo:

* Easy
* Medium
* Hard
* Brutal

Difficulty should affect decision quality, reaction, frequency, and tactical reliability, not whether the mechanic is available.

Harder CPUs should generally exploit good Mash Combo openings more effectively.

Do not guarantee that every CPU uses Mash Combo constantly.

A CPU must not gain a stronger version of the mechanic than the human player.

Opponent awareness

CPU intelligence must also understand an opponent currently performing Mash Combo.

It should recognize:

* The opponent’s commitment to a non-cancellable sequence.
* The rapid incoming attack pressure.
* That the first eleven turns have zero effective Base Launch.
* That the final turn restores normal launch.
* That the opponent can be interrupted.
* That Shield and positioning remain meaningful defenses.

Update relevant candidate evaluation, threat prediction, and opponent modeling so CPU decisions are based on Mash Combo’s real mechanics.

Game modes

Confirm Mash Combo works in:

* Regular Play.
* Custom Play.
* CPU versus player.
* CPU versus CPU Watch Mode.
* Mirror matches.
* Practice Ground for the player.

Preserve the Practice Ground’s existing non-attacking dummy behavior unless a separate attacking CPU is explicitly used.

No mode should require a character-specific hack.

